2186230117 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230167 #edb/ref 1064 "In many real-world situations, such as in retail or finance, it is necessary to apply multiple percentages in succession.\n\nTo do so, we perform one percentage calculation and then apply another percentage to the *result* of the previous calculation.\nThis means each new percentage is based on a *new amount*, not on the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $40\\%$ of $150,$ and then $25\\%$ of the result.\n\nFirst, we find $40\\%$ of $150{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40%\\times 150 & =\\frac{40}{100}\\times 150 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 150 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after the first step, the result is $60.$\nThen, we find $25\\%$ of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25%\\times 60 & =\\frac{25}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =0.25\\times 60 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after applying both percentages in succession, the final answer is $15.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230167 #edb/ref 1065 14335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230167 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81602213-1c20-5c8a-86ba-be4dd5c94554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230167 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230171 #edb/ref 1064 "When percentages are applied in succession, it is useful to represent their combined effect as a single percentage of the original amount. \n\nThis means that, instead of applying one percentage and then another, we can multiply their decimal multipliers to find an overall multiplier (or percentage) for the total change. \n\nFor example, suppose that a city’s library membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Of these members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. What percentage of last year’s members borrowed digital books?\n\nFirst, let $x$ be the number of members in the previous year.\n\nWe're told that membership rose to $150\\%$ of the previous year’s total. Thus, the new number of members is \n\n$$\n1.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are also told that of those members, $40\\%$ borrowed digital books. So, the number of members who borrowed digital books is\n\n$$\n0.4(1.5x) = 0.6x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.6x$ represents $60\\%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $60\\%$ of last year’s members borrowed digital books." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230171 #edb/ref 1065 14343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230171 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7bc4fcd7-c5b4-530f-8d3e-668da5241ab4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230171 #edb/ref 1067 "Subsequent Percentage Applications as a Single Percentage" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485810 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485810 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485810 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17f93f9d-e765-5f23-b7a3-a6bf802d6bbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485838 #edb/ref 1080 "20%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485838 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485838 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "293d27cb-6be7-542a-a8b8-147db89da707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485875 #edb/ref 1080 "30%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485875 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485875 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39360d96-4ec6-5354-8205-5964ad5fd93c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485902 #edb/ref 1080 "1.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485902 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485902 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ba290ae-ce34-542b-8844-2cd913434a0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485920 #edb/ref 1080 "30%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485920 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485920 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "57944d59-4548-51e3-b2bc-f10d7935a6a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485921 #edb/ref 1080 "45%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485921 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485921 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58e2fcd0-4ea8-5bae-941b-1c3addb5d792" #edb