17592186045417] [17592186486274 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486274 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486274 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486274 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486274 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "54555888-2f4a-506a-879f-422748de22f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486279 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486279 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486279 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486279 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486279 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6430fa47-fc07-5133-b350-c0c171fa758d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486339 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486339 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486339 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486339 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486339 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486339 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486339 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486339 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486339 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efb48e7b-fc34-5e8f-aab9-f0af854abc24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486368 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486368 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486368 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486368 #edb/ref 1071 "An engineer increased a battery’s capacity to $135%$ of its original rating. Then, a safety reserve equal to $20%$ of that new capacity was set aside. If the original capacity was $4000$ mAh, what is the safety reserve in mAh?\n\nThe safety reserve is  \n{{field-1}}\nmAh." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486368 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "311706e9-5725-57d0-9ebd-1cb5c9f7c783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486368 #edb/ref 1074 "First, the new capacity is $135%$ of the original rating $(4000$ mAh$):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}135%\\times 4000 & =\\frac{135}{100}\\times 4000 \\\\  & =1.35\\times 4000 \\\\  & =5400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the new capacity is $5400$ mAh. Then, the safety reserve is $20%$ of this new capacity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20%\\times 5400 & =\\frac{20}{100}\\times 5400 \\\\  & =0.20\\times 5400 \\\\  & =1080\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the safety reserve is $1080$ mAh." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486374 #edb/ref 1071 "Fitness class enrollment grew to $180%$ of the baseline. Of those enrolled, $15%$ chose yoga. What percentage of the baseline chose yoga?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a5e4afd-b3bf-55da-bca0-9104ea0bafe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486374 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the baseline enrollment.\n\nWe're told that enrollment grew to $180%$ of the baseline. Thus, the new enrollment is $1.8x.$\n\nWe are also told that of those enrolled, $15%$ chose yoga. So, the number of people who chose yoga is\n\n$$\n0.15(1.8x)=0.27x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.27x$ represents $27%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $27%$ of the baseline chose yoga." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486403 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486403 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486403 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486403 #edb/ref 1071 "A company’s employee count increased to $125%$ of last year’s total. Of these employees, $20%$ work remotely. What percentage of last year’s total now work remotely?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486403 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "786bda33-d02b-5551-a3f5-7adb0032f21a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486403 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ be the number of employees last year.\n\nWe're told that the total number increased to $125%$ of last year’s total. Thus, the current number of employees is $1.25x.$\n\nWe are also told that of these employees, $20%$ work remotely. So, the number of employees working remotely is\n\n$$\n0.20(1.25x)=0.25x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.25x$ represents $25%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $25%$ of last year’s employees now work remotely." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486404 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486404 #edb/ref 1069 "q-328004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486404 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486404 #edb/ref 1071 "A museum’s attendance decreased to $80%$ of its previous level. Then, a promotion increased attendance by $25%.$ If the museum originally had $24,000$ attendees, how many attendees were added by the promotion?\n\nThe number of attendees added is  \n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486404 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e4472fc-358a-5367-8f2d-7c530ac4418c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486404 #edb/ref 1074 "First, the new attendance is $80%$ of the previous level $(24,000$ attendees$):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80%\\times 24,000 & =\\frac{80}{100}\\times 24,000 \\\\  & =0.8\\times 24,000 \\\\  & =19,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the new attendance is $19,200.$ Then, the attendees added by the promotion is $25%$ of this new attendance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25%\\times 19,200 & =\\frac{25}{100}\\times 19,200 \\\\  & =0.25\\times 19,200 \\\\  & =4,800\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of attendees added is $4,800.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486434 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486434 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $30%$ of $50,$ and then $20%$ of the result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bee016d0-6cd7-5398-ae5a-81f82544f72f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486434 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find $30%$ of $50:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30%\\times 50 & =\\frac{30}{100}\\times 50 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 50 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $20%$ of the result $(15):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20%\\times 15 & =\\frac{20}{100}\\times 15 \\\\  & =0.2\\times 15 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486452 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $110%$ of $80,$ and then $30%$ of the result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e29b2a6a-e1e7-5fde-b63b-dc98de03f40b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486452 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find $110%$ of $80:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}110%\\times 80 & =\\frac{110}{100}\\times 80 \\\\  & =1.1\\times 80 \\\\  & =88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $30%$ of the result $(88):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30%\\times 88 & =\\frac{30}{100}\\times 88 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 88 \\\\  & =26.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $26.4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
